      "text": "The slice has been at the museum since 1893.",
      "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-slice.html"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "line": "My top lay under about an inch of dust.",
    "facts": [
     {
      "id": "dust_layer",
      "text": "Before treatment the top was under a layer of dust about an inch thick, over cloudy old varnish and wax.",
      "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-slice.html"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "line": "Cleaning revealed my rings.",
    "facts": [
     {
      "id": "rings_revealed",
      "text": "Conservation work revealed the slice's many rings, which show how long the tree lived.",
      "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-slice.html"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "line": "Thirteen hundred years of rings, cut down to be seen.",
    "facts": [
     {
      "id": "age_height",
      "text": "The tree was 1,300 years old and 101 metres tall when it was felled in California.",
      "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-slice.html"
     },
     {
      "id": "rings_revealed",
      "text": "Conservation work revealed the slice's many rings, which show how long the tree lived.",
      "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-slice.html"
     },
     {
      "id": "felled_for_museums",
      "text": "The Mark Twain Tree was felled so that sections of the trunk could be shown in museums in New York and London.",
      "source": "https://theplosblog.plos.org/2018/05/mark-twain-and-the-big-stump-can-we-save-nature-from-ourselves/"
     }
    ]
   }
  ],
  "web_facts": [
   {
    "id": "conservation_2016",
    "text": "The conservation work was carried out in 2016.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "origin_kings_canyon",
    "text": "The giant sequoia came from what is now Kings Canyon National Park, California.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "two_sections",
    "text": "Two sections were cut for display: the bottom, slightly larger one went to the AMNH.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "twelve_pieces",
    "text": "The top section was split into 12 pieces (one central disc and 11 radial segments) so it could be shipped to the UK.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "arrived_april_1893",
    "text": "The pieces arrived at South Kensington in April 1893.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "first_display",
    "text": "The section went on display the following year in one of the bays of the central hall.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "moves",
    "text": "The section was moved in 1902 to stand against the wall between the north and central halls, and again in 1971 to its current place on the second-floor balcony.",
    "source": "https://natsca.blog/2020/04/23/giant-sequoia-at-the-natural-history-museum/"
   },
   {
    "id": "mark_twain_tree_board",
    "text": "A museum interpretation board says the slice was taken from the Mark Twain tree.",
    "source": "https://www.thehallofeinar.com/2023/02/giant-sequoias/"
   },
   {
    "id": "felled_for_museums",
    "text": "The Mark Twain Tree was felled so that sections of the trunk could be shown in museums in New York and London.",
    "source": "https://theplosblog.plos.org/2018/05/mark-twain-and-the-big-stump-can-we-save-nature-from-ourselves/"
   },
   {
    "id": "felling_13_days",
    "text": "Felling the Mark Twain Tree took 13 days.",
    "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Twain_Tree"
   },
   {
    "id": "big_stump",
    "text": "The Mark Twain Tree's stump survives in Big Stump Grove and is known as the 'Big Stump' or 'Mark Twain Stump'.",
    "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Mark_Twain_Tree"
   },
   {
    "id": "species_range",
    "text": "Giant sequoias grow in the Sierra Nevada of California. They reach 94 metres tall with diameters of up to eight metres or a bit more, and individual trees have been dated to more than 3,000 years old.",
    "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-sequoiadendron-giganteum-worlds-biggest-tree.html"
   },
   {
    "id": "sequoia_name",
    "text": "The genus name Sequoia was coined in the mid-1840s by the Austrian botanist Stephan Endlicher, who gave no reason for the name. In the 1860s a Yosemite guidebook popularised the idea that he meant to honour Sequoyah, who invented the Cherokee syllabary.",
    "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-sequoiadendron-giganteum-worlds-biggest-tree.html"
   },
   {
    "id": "wellingtonia",
    "text": "In Britain, Lindley named the tree Wellingtonia after the Duke of Wellington.",
    "source": "https://www.nhm.ac.uk/discover/giant-sequoia-sequoiadendron-giganteum-worlds-biggest-tree.html"
   }
  ],
